  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe upper level pattern will continue to feature a trough over \nthe central CONUS as the ridge over the east remains in place \nbut begins to weaken as it shifts to the east-southeast with \ntime. The now closed low at the base of the trough over the \ncentral plains now opens and ejects a shortwave into the Lower \nOhio Valley. This will bring a cold front to the region entering\nthe Upper OH Valley by Thursday night. A weaker piece of energy\nmay travel the northern area of the ridge and bring some precip\nto the northern counties on Wednesday night.\n\nOtherwise, the main focus will be on the front attempting to\nmove through the region from the northwest. This will track east\nthrough the overnight and into the day on Friday. The rainfall\nthreat will not be anything to be concern over since we have\nbeen dry over the past week. The NBM suggest rainfall amounts\nwill not be too concerning as the probabilities of exceeding\nonly 0.50\" will be between 40% and 60% across the area. As well,\nthe thunderstorm threat on Thursday night and into Friday will\nbe minimal as even the NBM gives a 10% chance of even reaching\n500 J/Kg of SB CAPE. Even the prob thunder for this period is\nonly between 10% to 15%. Thus, nothing is worth mentioning in\nthe HWO at this time. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nBroad upper troughing is progged to set up across the NE CONUS\nthrough the upcoming weekend. Saturday should be mainly dry and\ncooler as surface high pressure builds in from the north, though\na crossing southern stream wave should result in shower chances\nmainly south of I-70. Shower chances then increase on Sunday as\na northern stream shortwave rotates through the broad upper\ntrough. Cooler temperatures are expected, with highs just below\nseasonable levels.",
